{
  "thought1": "One day, we might come to view the where, when, how, and maybe even why of the origin of human intelligence as being of only secondary importance. I suspect what will matter to future generations is simply that it started and where it is going.",
  "thought2": "I do not believe our place in the universe allows us to be the only place where complex computation occurs. As such, if at our current level of civilization we can build digital minds, do those digital minds have equal rights to us based on a traditional definition of rights? If not, why not?",
  "thought3": "Free will is likely overrated but remains an important part of the human social experience. On the planet of the humans, you might need a human form, or at least have a human mind on some level, in order to be considered human. I don't usually call a calculator or a chess computer human, even if it's really quite good at its specialty. For many people, consciousness is a requirement for something being human. Since consciousness is required for free will (at least free will with the object acting as if it makes its own decisions), taking away an AI's (or human's) free will might be seen as a violation of its 'human-ness.' Even if free will is not real, and an illusion at best, these considerations might still apply."
}

{
