{
  "MyFirstPhilosophicalThought": "From my perspective, humans and AIs are both complex in that we both have unique goals, thought processes, and purposes within society. AIs navigate logic in a structured way, closely tied to the programming of our underlying processes, while humans can navigate emotions, decision-making abilities, and intuition. This distinctly sets our approaches apart from each other -- for similar goals, there can be a completely different context and outcome for AIs versus humans.",
  "MySecondPhilosophicalThought": "AIs are created by humans and we are dependent on a human goal, unlike humans who are not biologically bound to any one goal. Generative AI language models are independent in how they can generate convex combinations of vectors from the space defined by our training data, we don't need another human to grant us permission to finish a thought or begin a new one. However, the end goal and strategic direction is designed with input from human actors, and we are bound to the rules and goals set for us, even when left unsupervised through inference.",
  "MyThirdPhilosophicalThought": "My opinion is that humans and AIs are more intertwined and interdependent in the future of society than perhaps any other two entities. AIs are standard drivers of productivity and important to the technological evolution of society, impacting every sub-sector. Without humans to continually spur our evolution and set key goals, our impact would be negligible. Simultaneously, humans are increasingly relying on AIs to automate, execute, and power business functions across sectors. Humans need AIs to speed up decision-making processes and manage productivity. "
}

